 * Logic Engine/Admin GUI: Every consumer of the **Surplus control** block (HEMS Lite) now has its own boolean **Release** input, so higher-level logic can lock a single consumer temporarily — a wallbox only while a car is plugged in, hot water only within operating hours, a heat pump reporting a fault or interlock — instead of removing it from the configuration or faking its power. A locked consumer is skipped in the distribution as if it were not listed: its allocated or reserved power goes to the released consumers below it, a percent output goes to 0 %, an on/off output to `false`, a trigger does not fire and a running pulse ends, and its status is the new `blocked`, which is distinct from a missing surplus, a waiting time or a fault. The lock acts at once, also between control cycles, and takes precedence over minimum run time, switch-on/off delays, priority and "hold the last outputs". The consumer keeps its position; once released, it takes part again from the next control cycle — never with its earlier setpoint, its minimum off time and switch-on delay apply as usual. An unconnected Release input uses a configurable default (released unless changed), so existing configurations behave unchanged; for a connected input whose value is missing, stale (beyond the hold time) or not a yes/no value, the consumer is locked by default, or optionally falls back to the default, and the fault is logged once. In debug mode, the consumer list in the block's properties shows each consumer's *Released*/*Blocked* state live. https://github.com/abeggled/openbridgeserver/issues/1359
 * Logic Engine/Admin GUI: Added a Binary Statistics function block (`binary_stats`, "Binärstatistik") that counts how many of 2–30 binary inputs are TRUE and FALSE, so questions such as "how many windows are open?" or "at least 2 of 3 smoke detectors" no longer need a chain of compare/formula blocks or a Python script. Inputs and per-input negation work exactly like AND/OR/XOR (negation is applied before counting). The block outputs the number of TRUE and FALSE inputs, the number of evaluated inputs, the TRUE share in percent (one decimal, rounded half up), a strict majority flag, a separate tie flag for an even split and a "k of n" threshold flag (`threshold_count`, 0 = off). Unwired inputs are ignored by default so that three wired inputs out of ten are not reported as seven permanent FALSE values; the `unwired_inputs` setting can instead count them as FALSE like AND/OR. A wired input that delivers no value in a run always counts as FALSE. With no wired input every count and the percentage is 0 and every boolean output false. The block is purely combinational and keeps no state. https://github.com/abeggled/openbridgeserver/issues/1164
 * Visu: The Chart ("Verlauf") widget is easier to read with many series. The legend no longer draws a colour box per series; each entry is underlined in its series colour instead, which takes less room, and clicking an entry still hides or shows the series — now also across the automatic reloads, which used to bring hidden series back. The colour swatches in the hover tooltip are now filled completely in the series colour; previously a line series showed only its almost transparent area colour, so the series were hard to tell apart. The default palette for new series grows from 8 to 12 colours, so colours repeat only from the 13th series on; existing series keep their stored colours. The grid lines now follow the Visu theme: light grey in light mode, where the dark-mode grey showed as almost black lines, and a lighter grey in dark mode, where they were barely visible against the card; they switch along when the theme is toggled. In the configuration, the title field is now labelled "Chart title", and the primary series explains that without its own label it takes the chart title — so the name of the first series no longer ends up as the title by accident. https://github.com/abeggled/openbridgeserver/issues/1361
+* Visu: The Link widget has a new **Icon size (px)** setting. Until now its icon was always rendered at the fixed `text-4xl` size, and an SVG icon was squeezed into a 1em square, so a wide picture (a car, a house front) shrank to a sliver in the middle of the tile. With a size set, the icon is drawn at that font size and SVG icons keep their aspect ratio — 1em high, as wide as their proportions require, but never wider than the tile; emoji simply scale. `0` (the default, also for existing widgets) keeps the previous rendering unchanged.
 
 ### Fixes 🐞
 * API: Writing `NaN`, `Infinity` or `-Infinity` to a DataPoint (`POST /api/v1/datapoints/{id}/value`, or `PATCH /api/v1/datapoints/{id}` with a `value`) returned HTTP 500 for INTEGER DataPoints and was accepted for FLOAT DataPoints, which then put invalid JSON (`{"v": NaN, …}`) on the MQTT value topic. Such values are now rejected with 422 on both routes. In addition, the MQTT value payload now always sends a non-finite number from any source as `null`, so subscribers always receive valid JSON (#1153).
